  "content": {
    "id": "p33-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Consider functions $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ that satisfy $f(f(x))=x$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb R$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p33-q11a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that if $f$ satisfies the above condition, then the function must be injective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $f(x)=f(y)$, applying $f$ gives $x=y$. Hence $f$ is injective.",
          "worked": "Suppose $f(x)=f(y)$. Applying $f$ to both sides gives\n$$\\begin{align*}f(f(x))&=f(f(y))\\\\x&=y.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $f$ is injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q11b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose $f$ satisfies the above condition and is strictly increasing. Prove that $f(x)=x$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb R$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)=x$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\nEither strict inequality between $f(x)$ and $x$ is reversed by the self-inverse condition, contradicting strict increase.",
          "worked": "Fix $x\\in\\mathbb R$. If $f(x)>x$, strict increase gives $f(f(x))>f(x)$, so $x>f(x)$, a contradiction.\n\nIf $f(x)<x$, strict increase gives $f(f(x))<f(x)$, so $x<f(x)$, again a contradiction.\n\nThus $f(x)=x$ for every $x\\in\\mathbb R$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 49
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q11c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "There are many functions that satisfy the above condition and that are strictly decreasing. For example, show that this is true for all functions of the form $f(x)=k-x$, for any fixed $k\\in\\mathbb R$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(f(x))=k-(k-x)=x$. If $a<b$, then $k-a>k-b$, so $f$ is strictly decreasing.",
          "worked": "For every $x\\in\\mathbb R$,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(f(x))&=k-(k-x)\\\\f(f(x))&=x.\\end{align*}$$\nIf $a<b$, then $k-a>k-b$, so $f(a)>f(b)$. Therefore $f$ is strictly decreasing and satisfies the required condition for every fixed $k\\in\\mathbb R$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p33-q11d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Give an example of a function that satisfies the above condition, but which is neither strictly increasing, nor strictly decreasing.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(x)=\\begin{cases}x,&x\\in\\mathbb Q,\\\\-x,&x\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\mathbb Q.\\end{cases}$\n\nRational inputs are fixed; irrational inputs are negated twice. The function is neither strictly increasing nor strictly decreasing.",
          "worked": "Take\n$$f(x)=\\begin{cases}x,&x\\in\\mathbb Q,\\\\-x,&x\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\mathbb Q.\\end{cases}$$\nFor rational $x$, $f(f(x))=f(x)=x$. For irrational $x$, $-x$ is irrational, and\n$$\\begin{align*}f(f(x))&=f(-x)\\\\f(f(x))&=-(-x)\\\\f(f(x))&=x.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $1<\\sqrt2$ but $f(1)=1>f(\\sqrt2)=-\\sqrt2$, the function is not strictly increasing. Since $0<1$ but $f(0)=0<f(1)=1$, it is not strictly decreasing.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
  },
